        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1. Because the most important event in Thucydides's History of the Peloponnesian War is Athens's catastrophic Sicilian Invasion, Thucydides devotes three-quarters of his book to setting it up. We can see how he anticipates it in how he describes the step-by-step decline in Athenian society, creating the inevitability we associate with tragic drama.",
